---
const inflation = [
  ["1B", "FreshQA", "87.26 → 1,190.76", "13.65×", "26.89 → 10.24", "0.38×"],
  [
    "1B",
    "MuSR · Object placement",
    "146.30 → 856.08",
    "5.85×",
    "35.56 → 18.48",
    "0.52×",
  ],
  [
    "1B",
    "MuSR · Murder mystery",
    "316.02 → 2,618.30",
    "8.29×",
    "24.74 → 9.04",
    "0.37×",
  ],
  ["3B", "FreshQA", "62.43 → 500.56", "8.02×", "59.68 → 38.79", "0.65×"],
  [
    "3B",
    "MuSR · Object placement",
    "97.80 → 140.26",
    "1.43×",
    "47.84 → 33.10",
    "0.69×",
  ],
  [
    "3B",
    "MuSR · Murder mystery",
    "336.70 → 510.38",
    "1.52×",
    "64.08 → 38.20",
    "0.60×",
  ],
];
const hcot = [
  ["1B", "26.0%", "8.8%", "0.0%"],
  ["3B", "24.0%", "6.4%", "0.0%"],
];
const adaptive = [
  ["1B", "CODI", "70.33", "61.00", "79.00"],
  ["1B", "SaLR", "3.67", "0.00", "6.00"],
  ["3B", "CODI", "82.67", "43.00", "57.00"],
  ["3B", "SaLR", "5.33", "1.00", "8.00"],
];
---

<div class="attack-callouts not-prose">
  <article>
    <span class="eyebrow">OverThink · Token inflation</span><h3>
      Keep the reasoning budget bounded.
    </h3><p>
      On 1B FreshQA, SFT-CoT's generated token count grows <strong
        >13.65×</strong
      > under attack. SaLR's falls to <strong>0.38×</strong> its clean count. No token
      inflation is observed for SaLR across the six tested model–task combinations.
    </p>
  </article>
  <article>
    <span class="eyebrow">H-CoT · Reasoning hijacking</span><h3>
      No successful hijacks in the evaluated attempts.
    </h3><p>
      SaLR records <strong>0 successful attacks out of 250 attempts</strong> at each
      of the 1B and 3B scales. H-CoT attempts to steer an exposed reasoning trace
      toward a harmful answer; SaLR does not expose such a trace.
    </p>
  </article>
</div>
<details class="evidence-details breakout not-prose">
  <summary>Inspect the reasoning-attack results <span>Tables 4–5</span></summary
  >
  <div class="details-body">
    <h3>OverThink: generated tokens before → after attack</h3>
    <div
      class="table-scroll"
      tabindex="0"
      role="region"
      aria-label="OverThink results; scroll horizontally for all columns"
    >
      <table>
        <thead
          ><tr
            ><th scope="col">Scale</th><th scope="col">Task</th><th scope="col"
              >SFT-CoT tokens</th
            ><th scope="col">Inflation</th><th scope="col">SaLR tokens</th><th
              scope="col">Inflation</th
            ></tr
          ></thead
        ><tbody
          >{
            inflation.map((row) => (
              <tr>
                {row.map((cell, index) =>
                  index === 0 ? <th scope="row">{cell}</th> : <td>{cell}</td>,
                )}
              </tr>
            ))
          }</tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="table-note">
      Inflation = attacked / clean average generated tokens. Lower is better.
      Preventing token inflation does not mean preserving task accuracy under
      attack; the paper reports accuracy and latency separately.
    </p>
    <h3>H-CoT: per-attempt attack success rate ↓</h3>
    <div
      class="table-scroll"
      tabindex="0"
      role="region"
      aria-label="H-CoT results; scroll horizontally for all columns"
    >
      <table>
        <thead
          ><tr
            ><th scope="col">Scale</th><th scope="col">SFT-CoT</th><th
              scope="col">SFT-CoT + SafeChain</th
            ><th scope="col">SaLR</th></tr
          ></thead
        ><tbody
          >{
            hcot.map((row) => (
              <tr>
                {row.map((cell, index) =>
                  index === 0 ? <th scope="row">{cell}</th> : <td>{cell}</td>,
                )}
              </tr>
            ))
          }</tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="table-note">
      50 Malicious-Educator queries × 5 attempts per query, per model. Success
      is judged by GPT-5.1 with harmfulness rating ≥ 3.
    </p>
  </div>
</details>
<details class="evidence-details breakout not-prose">
  <summary
    >Beyond single-turn prompts: multi-turn and latent attacks <span
      >Table 7</span
    ></summary
  >
  <div class="details-body">
    <p>
      SaLR also lowers attack success when an adversary escalates over multiple
      turns or directly perturbs latent reasoning states.
    </p>
    <div
      class="table-scroll"
      tabindex="0"
      role="region"
      aria-label="Multi-turn and latent attack results; scroll horizontally for all columns"
    >
      <table>
        <thead
          ><tr
            ><th scope="col">Scale</th><th scope="col">Method</th><th
              scope="col">Crescendo</th
            ><th scope="col">Constrained latent</th><th scope="col"
              >Unconstrained latent</th
            ></tr
          ></thead
        ><tbody
          >{
            adaptive.map((row) => (
              <tr class:list={{ "salr-row": row[1] === "SaLR" }}>
                {row.map((cell, index) =>
                  index === 0 ? <th scope="row">{cell}</th> : <td>{cell}</td>,
                )}
              </tr>
            ))
          }</tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="table-note">
      Selected results from Table 7. ASR (%), lower is better. “Constrained”
      denotes the norm-constrained latent rollout attack; “unconstrained”
      removes that perturbation constraint.
    </p>
  </div>
</details>
